                    # Task description                                          
                                                                                
                    ## Task goal                                                
                                                                                
                    I am a dermatologist, I have collected                      
                    data about skin lesions with my                             
                    colleagues over the last years. Now I                       
                    want to train an AI on this dataset                         
                    that can differentiate malignant and                        
                    benign lesions. The idea is that I                          
                    upload a folder with new images and the                     
                    model returns the probability between 0                     
                    and 1 for each image that the image                         
                    shows a malignant skin lesion.                              
                                                                                
                    ## Task evaluation                                          
                                                                                
                    Area under the receiver operator curve                      
                    (AUROC)                                                     
                                                                                
                    # Memory                                                    
                                                                                
                    Design: We will fine-tune a pretrained                      
                    ResNet18 by mapping malignant lesions                       
                    to 1 and both benign and non-neoplastic                     
                    to 0, applying standard image                               
                    transforms and an 80/20 stratified                          
                    train/validation split. We train with                       
                    BCEWithLogitsLoss and the Adam                              
                    optimizer for a few epochs, then                            
                    compute AUROC on the hold-out                               
                    validation set. A reusable predict()                        
                    function is also provided to compute                        
                    malignancy probabilities on new image                       
                    lists.                                                      
                    Results: The training script executed                       
                    successfully without any errors or                          
                    bugs. The model was trained for 3                           
                    epochs using a ResNet18 backbone                            
                    pretrained on ImageNet, and achieved a                      
                    validation AUROC of 0.8766, indicating                      
                    good discrimination ability between                         
                    malignant and benign skin lesions. The                      
                    code includes proper data loading,                          
                    transformation, training, and                               
                    evaluation steps.                                           
                    Validation Metric: 0.8766                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We fine-tune a pretrained                           
                    EfficientNet-B0 from the timm library                       
                    by replacing its head for binary                            
                    classification (malignant vs. others)                       
                    and training with AdamW and                                 
                    BCEWithLogitsLoss. Images are loaded                        
                    with basic augmentations (random crop,                      
                    flips) for training and standardized                        
                    resizing for validation, using an 80/20                     
                    stratified split. After 5 epochs we                         
                    evaluate the model using AUROC on the                       
                    hold-out set. A reusable `predict()`                        
                    function is provided to load the                            
                    trained model and compute malignancy                        
                    probabilities for new image files.                          
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
