                    EfficientNet-B0 from the timm library                       
                    by replacing its head for binary                            
                    classification (malignant vs. others)                       
                    and training with AdamW and                                 
                    BCEWithLogitsLoss. Images are loaded                        
                    with basic augmentations (random crop,                      
                    flips) for training and standardized                        
                    resizing for validation, using an 80/20                     
                    stratified split. After 5 epochs we                         
                    evaluate the model using AUROC on the                       
                    hold-out set. A reusable `predict()`                        
                    function is provided to load the                            
                    trained model and compute malignancy                        
                    probabilities for new image files.                          
                    Results: The training script ran                            
                    successfully without any errors. The                        
                    model, EfficientNet-B0, was fine-tuned                      
                    on the skin lesion dataset with                             
                    appropriate data augmentations and                          
                    normalization. The validation AUROC                         
                    improved steadily over 5 epochs,                            
                    reaching a best score of 0.9037,                            
                    indicating good discriminative                              
                    performance for malignant vs benign                         
                    lesion classification.                                      
                    Validation Metric: 0.9037                                   
                                                                                
                    -------------------------------                             
                    Design: We can improve generalization                       
                    and boost AUROC by incorporating Mixup                      
                    data augmentation during training,                          
                    which blends pairs of images and their                      
                    labels to regularize the model. We'll                       
                    implement Mixup in the training loop                        
                    with an alpha of 0.4, modifying                             
                    `train_one_epoch` to mix inputs and                         
                    targets and compute a weighted loss.                        
                    The rest of the pipeline (data loading,                     
                    model, optimizer) remains unchanged.                        
                    This atomic change should help the                          
                    model better generalize to unseen                           
                    lesions.                                                    
